DESCRIPTION:Faculty of Law\nTopics: Law\, International\nDetails: https://w
 ww.law.cam.ac.uk/events/lcil-friday-lecture-beckoning-choices-theory-autho
 rity-international-law-prof-fuad-zarbiyev\nDate/time: \n\n Friday 27 Novem
 ber 2026 \n\n 1:00pm to 2:00pm\n\n Location: \n\n Lauterpacht Centre for I
 nternational Law\n\n Berkowitz/Finley Lecture Hall\n\n About \n\n Register
  here if attending online   Situations in which claims to authority clash
  with each other abound in international law\, ranging from competition fo
 r authority among international adjudicative bodies to the disputed alloca
 tion of powers among international and domestic authorities across various
  issue areas. What is common to all these situations is that each claim to
  authority is typically supported by some formal or informal basis for eli
 citing deference. Yet the practice shows that not all claims to deference 
 entitlement are situated on the same plane\, with some exerting greater at
 traction than others. Exclusively mandate-based authority claims\, for ins
 tance\, rarely gain the upper hand over epistemically grounded claims to d
END:VEVENT
