  is supported by the Cambridge Political Economy Society and the Economics
  and Policy Group at the Cambridge Judge Business School. \n\n Speaker Ove
 rview: \n Shailaja Fennell is Professor of Economic Security and Resilienc
 e in the Department of Land Economy. She obtained her B.A\, M. A. and M.Ph
 il. in Economics from Delhi University\, before coming to the University o
 f Cambridge to obtain another M.Phil. and then her PhD. in Economics at th
 e Faculty of Economics. She is a fellow of Jesus College\, and also Direct
 or of the Centre of South Asian Studies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:talks-273463@cambridge-events
DTSTAMP:20261118T073026Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261203T171500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261203T190000
SUMMARY:Financing Foreign Policy: UK Government–Bank Relations During the
  Development of the 1981–2 Sovereign Debt Crisis in Poland\, Romania and
  Hungary
LOCATION:Lightfoot Room - St John's College
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273463/
CATEGORIES:Politics,International,Policy
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Catherine Lefevre\, Glasgow\nCore Seminar in Economic 
 and Social History\nTopics: Politics\, International\, Policy\nDetails: ht
 tps://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273463/\nThis talk demonstrates how UK ba
 nks helped finance the UK’s foreign policy toward Central and Eastern Eu
 ropean (CEE) during the Cold War with the support and encouragement of the
  UK government. It examines the relationship between the UK government\, U
 K banks and the Bank of England during the development of the CEE sovereig
 n debt crisis in Poland\, Romania and Hungary and shows that UK banks play
 ed a key role in the development of East–West relations during the Cold 
 War through actively developing business ties with CEE countries which the
  UK government saw as an opportunity to further its foreign policy goals i
 n the region.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dept-www-law-cam-ac-uk-press-events-2026-12-lunchtime-conversation-what
 -special-@cambridge-events
DTSTAMP:20261118T073026Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261204T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261204T140000
SUMMARY:LCIL lunchtime conversation: 'What is Special about the Special Tri
 bunal for the Crime of Aggression against Ukraine?'
LOCATION:Lauterpacht Centre for International Law
URL:https://www.law.cam.ac.uk/press/events/2026/12/lunchtime-conversation-w
 hat-special-about-special-tribunal-crime-aggression-against-ukraine
CATEGORIES:Law,International,Criminology
DESCRIPTION:Faculty of Law\nTopics: Law\, International\, Criminology\nDeta
 ils: https://www.law.cam.ac.uk/press/events/2026/12/lunchtime-conversation
 -what-special-about-special-tribunal-crime-aggression-against-ukraine\nDat
 e/time: \n\n Friday 4 December 2026 \n\n 1:00pm to 2:00pm\n\n Location: \n
 \n Lauterpacht Centre for International Law\n\n Berkowitz/Finley Lecture H
 all\n\n About \n\n Register here if attending online   At a time when som
 e international courts and tribunals are under attack\, defunded or otherw
 ise obstructed\, a new one is being created: the Special Tribunal for the 
 Crime of Aggression against Ukraine. Ukrainian legal scholar Dr Gaiane Nur
 idzhanian \, in conversation with LCIL Fellow Professor Sarah Nouwen\, wil
 l explore what is special about the tribunal: why is it needed\, what is i
 ts legal nature\, what are its prospects and the role in the development o
 f international law? \n There is a sandwich lunch at 12.30 pm in the Old 
 Library at the Centre. All lecture attendees welcome.\n The Friday Lunchti
 me Lecture series is kindly supported by Cambridge University Press & Asse
 ssment .
END:VEVENT
