DTSTAMP:20261118T073026Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20270119T140000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20270119T150000
SUMMARY:Counterfeit Procedure: How 'Digital Arrest' Fraud Simulates the Sta
 te
LOCATION:Webinar & Lecture Theatre 2\, Computer Laboratory\, William Gates 
 Building
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273975/
CATEGORIES:Law,International
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Shakti Avasthy\, Indian Police Service\nComputer Labor
 atory Security Seminar\nTopics: Law\, International\nDetails: https://talk
 s.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/273975/\nIn the fraud known in India as 'digital ar
 rest'\, victims are told by people posing as officers of several state age
 ncies that they are under investigation\, shown forged orders of the count
 ry's highest court\, held under continuous video 'custody' for days\, and 
 instructed to move their savings into a 'supervised' account for verificat
 ion. Recorded complaints rose from about 40\,000 in 2022 to over 120\,000 
 in 2024. This talk argues that the offence is not impersonation but instit
 utional simulation: what the operators reproduce is not a person but the p
 rocedural form by which a state proceeds against a citizen - inter-agency 
 referral\, documentary authority\, staged adjudication\, custody - closely
  enough that compliance follows from recognising the procedure rather than
  believing any individual. Victims who verify are captured by the act of v
 erifying. Drawing on one case reconstructed from the 
END:VEVENT
